# ===========================================================================
# STAGE #2H - Resolving the last survivor
# ===========================================================================
#
# The specification written for the missing test turned out to be impossible - and finding out why was the result.
#
# This notebook is COMPLETE IN ITSELF. It does not depend on, and does not
# refer you to, any other notebook. Everything needed to reproduce, run,
# view and understand this stage is written out below.
#
# WHAT THIS STAGE WAS ABOUT
#
# The previous stage left one mutation surviving - the Zeno escalation
# threshold - and SPECIFIED the test it needed: a chain of SEQUENTIAL
# near-simultaneous impacts that must stay elastic, 'a Newton's cradle,
# most likely'.
#
# BUILDING IT SHOWED THE SPECIFICATION WAS WRONG, and that is the finding.
#
# A Newton's cradle does not produce such a chain. Its spheres TOUCH, so
# every contact is flagged in a single root return and the solver batches
# them into ONE event carrying several contacts. Separating them into
# distinct events would need time gaps under 1e-9 - which at any sane speed
# is below the contact tolerance, i.e. still touching.
#
# So bursts above one arise ONLY in true Zeno behaviour, where any threshold
# between 1 and 64 produces the same intended plastic result. The constant's
# exact value is genuinely unconstrained - and that is a DIFFERENT THING
# from an untested guard.
#
# THE FIX WAS TO ASK A BETTER QUESTION. The number 64 is a tuning choice;
# what needs pinning is the guard's BEHAVIOUR at both ends:
#
#     threshold = 0            fires on every impact       CAUGHT
#     threshold = usize::MAX   never fires at all          CAUGHT
#
# Both ends nailed down. That is a more useful statement than 'the threshold
# is tested' would have been, and it is true.
#
# FOUR MORE CRITICAL LINES were added to the probe and all were caught:
# the complex gamma function's Stirling argument shift, complex Airy's
# series/asymptotic switch, the constant Ai(0) with four digits dropped, and
# the restitution threshold. Score: 18 of 19.
#
# The single remaining survivor is a documented EQUIVALENT MUTANT - inside
# the guarded sector two different branch-anchor coefficients unwrap to the
# same branch, so no input in the served domain can distinguish them.
#
# THE UNIQUENESS RULE EARNED ITS KEEP AGAIN. Every mutation anchor must
# match exactly once or the run aborts. It fired twice more here: once on an
# anchor matching TWO lines - the two integrator paths carry the same guard,
# which is itself worth knowing, since a fix applied to one and not the
# other is exactly the earlier granularity hazard - and once on zero.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN THIS NOTEBOOK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything below is posim notebook source. Lines beginning with '#' are
# comments and are ignored by the interpreter; every other line is a command
# that will be executed in order.
#
# From the repository root (the directory holding Cargo.toml), run ONE of:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook StageNbks/Stage_2H.posim
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --script   StageNbks/Stage_2H.posim
#
#   --notebook  prints an In[n]:= / Out[n]= transcript, like a Mathematica
#               notebook. This is the mode to use when reading along.
#   --script    prints the same transcript; the two differ only in how the
#               prompt lines are laid out. Either is reproducible.
#
# To work through it by hand instead, start the interactive interpreter:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release
#
# and paste the command lines one at a time. Type HELP for the full command
# language, and %quit to leave.
#
# First run will compile the workspace (a few minutes). Later runs are
# immediate. If 'cargo' is not found, install Rust from https://rustup.rs .
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCHING THE BROWSER GUI TO INVESTIGATE THIS STAGE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# posim ships a live 3-D scene window that is served over HTTP and viewed in
# an ordinary web browser. There is no separate application to install.
#
# COMPLETE PROCEDURE, from nothing to a moving picture:
#
#   1. Open a terminal at the repository root and start posim interactively:
#
#          cargo run -p posim --release
#
#      You will get the banner and an 'In[1]:=' prompt.
#
#   2. Build a scene. You must create bodies BEFORE opening the window,
#      because the window shows the entities that exist when it is created.
#      (The command lines further down this notebook do exactly that.)
#
#   3. Open the window:
#
#          scene create
#
#      posim replies with, for example:
#
#          scene window created: http://127.0.0.1:41234/
#          (opened in your browser; if no window appeared, open that address
#           yourself)
#          showing 4 entities; SCENE START begins the evolution
#
#      The port is chosen freely each run. To pin it, pass one:
#
#          scene create 8080
#
#      then the address is always http://127.0.0.1:8080/ .
#
#   4. If no browser opened by itself, copy the printed address into any
#      browser (Firefox, Chrome, Safari, Edge - it is plain HTML, CSS and
#      JavaScript over a websocket; nothing else is required).
#
#   5. Drive the animation from the SAME posim prompt while the page is open:
#
#          scene start          begin evolving
#          scene pause          freeze
#          scene stop           halt
#          scene reverse        play backwards through the snapshot history
#          scene set_time_step 0.001      seconds of simulated time per frame
#          scene refresh        re-read the body list after adding bodies
#          scene close          shut the window and free the port
#
#      In the page itself: drag with the mouse to orbit, scroll to zoom,
#      shift-drag or the arrow keys to pan, and the on-screen buttons mirror
#      the commands above.
#
#   6. HELP lists every scene command with its arguments.
#
# TROUBLESHOOTING, precisely:
#   * 'no scene window - run SCENE CREATE first' means step 3 was skipped.
#   * A blank page means no bodies existed at 'scene create' time: add them,
#     then issue 'scene refresh'.
#   * If the port is busy, 'scene close' then 'scene create <other port>'.
#   * The window is local only (127.0.0.1). It is not reachable from another
#     machine, by design.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANIMATING THIS STAGE - FULLY POSSIBLE FOR PARTS 1 AND 2
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Both rigid-body scenarios animate properly. Part 3 (the shell harness)
# cannot be animated: it has no bodies and no simulated time, so the scene
# window would show an empty grid.
#
# COMPLETE PROCEDURE for the Newton's cradle:
#
#   1. cargo run -p posim --release
#
#   2. Paste the PART 1 lines above, down to and including the last
#      'new sphere { ... }' line. Do not run 'run' yet.
#
#   3. scene create
#      posim replies with an address such as
#          scene window created: http://127.0.0.1:41234/
#      and normally opens it in your browser. If no window appeared, paste
#      that address into any browser yourself. To pin the port instead:
#          scene create 8080      ->  http://127.0.0.1:8080/
#
#   4. scene set_time_step 0.002
#      scene zoom 2
#      scene start
#
#   5. Watch the impulse cross the chain and only the far sphere depart.
#      Drag to orbit, scroll to zoom, shift-drag or arrow keys to pan.
#      'scene pause' freezes it; 'scene reverse' plays it backwards through
#      the snapshot history, which is worth doing on a cradle.
#
#   6. For PART 2, 'reset', paste the settling-ball lines, then repeat
#      steps 3-5 with  scene set_time_step 0.005 . Watch the bounces
#      shorten geometrically and the ball come to rest ON the floor rather
#      than sinking through it - that is the Zeno guard working.
#
#   7. scene close   when finished.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECREATING THE STAGE SCENARIO
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PART 1 - the Newton's cradle that motivated the specification. An incomer
# strikes a chain of four touching spheres; the impulse must propagate so
# that ONLY the far sphere leaves, at the incomer's speed.
#
# This is also the demonstration that the contacts are SIMULTANEOUS: the
# whole chain resolves inside one event, which is precisely why it could not
# raise the burst counter.
reset
set system.g_constant = 0
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [-3, 0, 0], velocity = [1, 0, 0] }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [0, 0, 0] }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [1, 0, 0] }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [2, 0, 0] }
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [3, 0, 0] }

# Total energy and momentum before the strike.
energy
momentum

run 6 steps 60

# After: only the last sphere should be moving, at the incomer's speed,
# with energy and momentum unchanged.
energy
momentum
get obj0.velocity
get obj4.velocity

# PART 2 - a settling ball, which IS genuine Zeno behaviour: infinitely many
# impacts in finite time. The guard must fire here, and the run must
# terminate and come to rest rather than looping forever.
reset
set system.g_constant = 0
new cuboid { mass = 1, half_extents = [5, 0.5, 5], position = [0, -0.5, 0] }
set obj0.inverse_mass = 0
new sphere { mass = 1, radius = 0.5, position = [0, 2, 0], restitution = 0.5 }
set system.uniform_gravity = [0, -10, 0]
run 6 steps 60
get obj1.position
get obj1.velocity

# PART 3 - the probe itself, from a normal shell at the repository root:
#
#     bash scripts/mutation_probe.sh zeno
#
# Expect all three Zeno mutations CAUGHT: the burst window, the guard always
# firing, and the guard never firing.

# ===========================================================================
# END OF STAGE #2H NOTEBOOK
# ===========================================================================
